// EXPECT: PASS
// Phase 2 slice 1, REGRESSION GUARD for a FALSE REJECTION this slice originally introduced.
//
// This program is legitimate. `Plain::tag(self, s)` is called as `p.tag(p)`, so `s` is a `Plain`
// whose `k` is a public `i64`; printing it is fine.
//
// The first version of the call-site place-type inference rejected it with
// ANUBIS_SECRET_EXFILTRATION. `param_place_candidates` is keyed by BARE NAME and is filled only
// from `Expr::Call` sites — free-function calls. The free `tag(p, b)` recorded `Box` in slot 1, and
// the impl method `tag`, whose own formal `s` also sits at index 1, inherited it. A `Box.k` is
// declared `secret<i64>`, so the method's perfectly public field read was charged as an
// exfiltration.
//
// That is `AGENTS.md`'s standing rule, violated: "Never merge namespaces. A map keyed by a bare
// method name that a free function can also claim is a four-times-proven defect generator here."
// Methods now receive no hint from that map at all. Extending the inference to methods needs its
// own method-keyed candidate map, which is separate work.
//
// Found by review, not by the corpus — every existing fixture passed. It is committed so the next
// person who widens this inference has to keep the namespaces apart.
struct Box { k: secret<i64> }
struct Plain { k: i64 }

fn tag(a, s) { return a; }

impl Plain {
    fn tag(self, s) { print(s.k); }
}

fn main() {
    let p = Plain { k: 1 };
    let b = Box { k: 42 };
    let n = tag(p, b);
    p.tag(p);
}
